{"analysis":"The mixed‐effects model (n=137,262, 245 reefs) shows a highly significant negative effect of area_deviation on biomass density: coefficient = –0.318 t/ha per m² (z=–76.2, p<0.001). Larger transect areas systematically underestimate density (and smaller areas overestimate it). Residuals vs. area_deviation are centered on zero (with few outliers), and the biomass_density vs. area_deviation scatter with OLS line confirms the negative bias. A simple correction is to adjust each transect’s density by:

    biomass_density_corrected = biomass_density – (–0.318219) × (area – 250)

This removes the linear bias due to area deviations.","success":true}

{"feedback":"The hypothesis—that deviation from the ideal 250 m² transect area systematically biases biomass density estimates—was directly tested. Biomass density was computed per transect, area deviation was defined (area – 250), and a mixed‐effects model (random intercept by reef; fixed effects: area_deviation, depth, mean SST) was fit to the data (n = 137 262). The coefficient for area_deviation is –0.318 t/ha per m² (z=–76.2, p<0.001), confirming that larger transect areas underestimate density (and smaller areas overestimate it). Residuals vs. area_deviation and a scatterplot with an OLS fit line both corroborate this linear bias. A bias‐correction guideline was provided: 

    biomass_density_corrected = biomass_density – (–0.318219) × (area – 250)

This fully addresses the experiment objectives and confirms the presence of systematic bias due to transect area deviations.","success":true}
